# Every package and every module keeps a short, current README

## Pyramid Layer

- Layer: L0 convention.

## Use This When

- A new workspace appears under `apps/`, `packages/`, `tests/`, `database/` or `seed/`.
- A new feature module appears under an application's `src/features/`.
- A change alters a package's role, its key exports or its commands, or a module's routes, shapes, commands or providers.

## Stop Here If

- The package's or module's `README.md` matches the shape below and still describes it as it is after your change.

## Core rule

- Every workspace has a `README.md` with exactly this skeleton, kept short:
  - `# <Title> (\`<package name>\`)`
  - `## Role in the architecture`: what the package is for and which layer it serves, in a few sentences.
  - `## Key exports`: the public entry points and subpaths.
  - `## Commands`: how to test and check it.
- Every feature module (a folder under `src/features/`) has its own `README.md`, kept short:
  - `# <Title> (module \`<id>\`)`, the id from its `*.module.ts` manifest (the folder name when it has none)
  - Folders beginning with `__` or `.` (`__fixtures__`, `__snapshots__`) are support folders, not modules.
  - `## Role in the architecture`: what the module is for and which layers it has.
  - `## Surface`: routes and pages, shapes, commands, providers, the i18n namespace and test ids.
  - `## Commands`: how to test and regenerate it.
- A gateway module (a folder under `apps/gateway/src/` with an `index.ts`, `entity-first-module-naming.mdc`) keeps a README of the same shape: `# <Title> (module \`<folder>\`)`, its role, its surface (routes, messages, services exported from the index) and its commands. The check covers web feature modules today; a gateway module's README is reviewed by hand until it does.
- A module's details belong to the module's README. The application's README keeps one line per module pointing there, so nothing piles up in the parent.
- Update the README in the same change that alters the role, exports, commands, routes, shapes or providers. A missing README is created, not deferred.
- Describe what exists, not plans.
- `bun run readme:check` (part of `bun run check`) fails on a missing README, a title that does not name the package or module, or a missing section.
